            ("What a flash is", "A **stepped leader** of negative charge zigzags down from the cloud in steps of about 50 m, and when it nears the ground a streamer reaches up to meet it. The channel is then connected and the bright **return stroke**, which carries current of about 30,000 amperes (up to 300,000), flows back up. A flash typically has 3 to 4 strokes in rapid succession, which is why it flickers."),
            ("Beyond the bolt", "Most lightning stays inside the cloud or runs from cloud to cloud. 'Megaflashes' run horizontally in the anvil, and the longest verified were more than 700 km long, from Brazil and the United States. Above the storm, short-lived flashes called sprites and blue jets reach up to the edge of space."),
